## Reader's guide

**Neuroevolution through Elixir**  
Part I - Foundations

**The central idea:** give independently acting components explicit identities,
state, and message interfaces. Then specify who detects failures, who restarts
components, and which information survives.

This chapter remasters Gene I. Sher's *Handbook of Neuroevolution Through Erlang*
(2013), Chapter 5, **"The Unintentional Neural Network Programming Language,"**
printed pp. 143-150. The source's Sections **5.1-5.6** and argument are retained.
References to **[S5]** use printed page numbers; PDF page 1 is printed p. 143.

Sher argues for Erlang because its process-oriented architecture closely matches
his neural-network architecture. His claims of a *perfect* mapping and an ideal
language are presented as **his architectural position**, not as a benchmark or
an established ranking of programming languages. [S5, pp. 143-148]

**Remaster additions:** all executable Elixir/Nx examples, implementation
qualifications, new diagrams, checks, and the Generation-Evaluation-Selection
(GES) connection. The original chapter contains an architectural argument and
one mapping figure, not the implementations below.

### Chapter map

| Section                                                          | Source question                                       | Remaster focus                                                      |
| ---------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| 5.1 The Necessary Features                                       | What should the language support?                     | Concurrency, communication, recovery, upgrades, hardware interfaces |
| 5.2 Erlang: From Telecommunications Networks To Neural Networks  | Why does a telecom language fit this problem?         | Erlang, Elixir, BEAM, and OTP                                       |
| 5.3 The Conceptual Mapping of a NN to Erlang's Architecture      | What corresponds to a neuron, connection, and signal? | Mailboxes, process identities, a small pipeline, Nx parity          |
| 5.4 Robustness and Fault Tolerance in Computational Intelligence | How can a system continue despite component failures? | Supervision, restart state, controlled code replacement             |
| 5.5 I've Done This Once Before; And We Are On Our Way            | What does the rest of the book build?                 | GES orchestration and isolated evaluation jobs                      |
| 5.6 References                                                   | What supports the chapter?                            | Source references and official implementation documentation         |

### Run this notebook

Evaluate the setup cell, then the remaining cells in order. Nothing from an
earlier notebook is required. Nx and Kino retain the versions used in Chapters
2-4. No chart package, GPU, native tensor backend, dataset, remote BEAM node, or
hardware device is required. Initial package installation needs network access
or a prepared package cache.

Only **computed data tables** have **Interactive / Markdown / CSV** tabs.
Explanatory tables remain ordinary Markdown. Every named function written in
this notebook has an `@spec`; a specification documents a contract, but is not
runtime input validation. [D8]

The demonstrations create short-lived local processes and stop them in `after`
blocks. They do not register global process names, start an indefinite service,
change logger settings, or open a network port. The code-replacement experiment
uses one dedicated module name and two fixed source strings.

**Expected diagnostics:** the failure demonstrations may print process or
supervisor reports; the code-replacement demonstration may print a module
redefinition warning. Their checks determine whether the intended behavior
occurred. A cell that raises an assertion or exits unexpectedly is not a pass.

**Validation status:** the authoring environment has no Elixir/BEAM runtime.
Source coverage, notebook structure, named-function specification coverage, and
independent numerical examples were checked. The actual Elixir/Nx execution,
process lifecycle behavior, and Livebook rendering have **not** been executed
here. The final cell checks those behaviors in your runtime.

### Shared support

`tbl_` names table rows; `mod_` names a model. Processes use descriptive `pid_`
names. Data-table CSV callbacks return **binary text**, not row maps. [D6, D7]

```elixir
Nx.default_backend(Nx.BinaryBackend)
Nx.Defn.default_options(compiler: Nx.Defn.Evaluator)

defmodule Chapter05.Tables do
  @moduledoc false
  @type row :: %{optional(atom()) => term()}

  @spec new([row()], [atom()], String.t()) :: Kino.Layout.t()
  def new(rows, columns, filename) do
    csv_text = csv(rows, columns)

    Kino.Layout.tabs([
      {"Interactive", Kino.DataTable.new(rows, keys: columns)},
      {"Markdown", Kino.Markdown.new(markdown(rows, columns))},
      {"CSV",
       Kino.Layout.grid([
         Kino.Download.new(fn -> csv_text end,
           filename: filename,
           label: "Download CSV"
         ),
         Kino.Text.new(csv_text)
       ], columns: 1)}
    ])
  end

  @spec csv([row()], [atom()]) :: binary()
  def csv(rows, columns) do
    records = [Enum.map(columns, &Atom.to_string/1) | values(rows, columns)]

    Enum.map_join(records, "\r\n", fn record ->
      Enum.map_join(record, ",", &csv_field/1)
    end) <> "\r\n"
  end

  @spec markdown([row()], [atom()]) :: String.t()
  def markdown(rows, columns) do
    records = [
      Enum.map(columns, &Atom.to_string/1),
      Enum.map(columns, fn _ -> "---" end)
      | values(rows, columns)
    ]

    Enum.map_join(records, "\n", fn record ->
      "| " <> Enum.map_join(record, " | ", &markdown_field/1) <> " |"
    end)
  end

  @spec values([row()], [atom()]) :: [[String.t()]]
  defp values(rows, columns) do
    Enum.map(rows, fn row ->
      Enum.map(columns, fn key -> row |> Map.fetch!(key) |> text() end)
    end)
  end

  @spec text(term()) :: String.t()
  defp text(nil), do: ""
  defp text(value) when is_binary(value), do: value
  defp text(value) when is_atom(value) or is_number(value), do: to_string(value)
  defp text(value), do: inspect(value, limit: :infinity)

  @spec csv_field(String.t()) :: String.t()
  defp csv_field(value), do: "\"" <> String.replace(value, "\"", "\"\"") <> "\""

  @spec markdown_field(String.t()) :: String.t()
  defp markdown_field(value) do
    value
    |> String.replace("\\", "\\\\")
    |> String.replace("&", "&amp;")
    |> String.replace("<", "&lt;")
    |> String.replace(">", "&gt;")
    |> String.replace("|", "\\|")
    |> String.replace("\r\n", "<br>")
    |> String.replace("\n", "<br>")
    |> String.replace("\r", "<br>")
  end
end

defmodule Chapter05.Check do
  @moduledoc false

  @spec row!({boolean(), String.t()}) :: %{check: String.t(), result: String.t()}
  def row!({true, label}), do: %{check: label, result: "PASS"}
  def row!({false, label}), do: raise("Check failed: #{label}")

  @spec down!(reference(), pid()) :: term()
  def down!(monitor, pid) do
    receive do
      {:DOWN, ^monitor, :process, ^pid, reason} -> reason
    after
      2_000 ->
        Process.demonitor(monitor, [:flush])
        raise "Timed out waiting for process termination"
    end
  end

  @spec started!(reference(), atom()) :: pid()
  def started!(tag, id) do
    receive do
      {:chapter05_started, ^tag, ^id, pid} -> pid
    after
      2_000 -> raise "Timed out waiting for #{inspect(id)} to start"
    end
  end
end
```

## 5.1 The Necessary Features

*Source: [S5], Section 5.1, pp. 144-146; opening motivation on p. 143.*

Sher asks us to design a language around the system we want to express.
His starting point is a network of independently acting neurons that communicate
through signals. He then adds the requirements of a long-running system:
recovery, live maintenance, distribution, and interaction with hardware.

| Requirement in Sher's argument                | What it makes possible                                      |
| --------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------- |
| Many independent concurrent units             | Represent neurons and other active components directly      |
| Messages between those units                  | Represent signals and connections                           |
| Failure detection and recovery                | Notice a failed component and restore service               |
| Code replacement during operation             | Change an implementation without stopping the entire system |
| Distribution across machines                  | Place communicating components on more than one computer    |
| Hardware interfaces                           | Connect sensors and actuators to the computational system   |
| Timing, reliability, and continuous operation | Coordinate a system that must keep serving its environment  |

Sher reinforces this list with telecommunications requirements attributed to
Bjarne Dacker: numerous concurrent activities, timing constraints, distribution,
hardware control, large and interacting software components, long uptime, live
maintenance, reliability, and fault tolerance. His point is that **telecom
systems already demanded much of the infrastructure he wants for neural systems**.
[S5, pp. 145-146]

### Remaster qualification - a capability is not a guarantee

A runtime feature and an application-level result are different things.

| Available mechanism  | The application still has to decide                                  |
| -------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Concurrent processes | Which work is independent, and which work must wait                  |
| Message passing      | Message format, ordering assumptions, request identity, and timeouts |
| Supervision          | Restart boundaries, restart limits, and state reconstruction         |
| Code loading         | Compatibility, state migration, testing, and rollback                |
| Distribution         | Placement, connection security, partitions, and retry semantics      |
| Hardware integration | Device protocols, safety limits, and handling unavailable devices    |

These are implementation qualifications added for the remaster, not a replacement
for Sher's feature list. Our examples demonstrate small local mechanisms; they
do not prove uninterrupted operation, hard real-time deadlines, or autonomous
bug repair. [D1-D5]

## 5.2 Erlang: From Telecommunications Networks To Neural Networks

*Source: [S5], Section 5.2, pp. 146-147.*

Sher describes Erlang as a concurrency-oriented language developed at Ericsson
for telecommunications. In his account, fault isolation, lightweight concurrency,
failure detection, location-transparent messaging, and dynamic code upgrades
provide the foundation. Storage, device interfaces, and operational libraries
complete the system. [S5, p. 146]

The practical lesson is to reuse an architecture built for many interacting,
long-lived components rather than rebuild that infrastructure inside every
neural-network experiment.

### Remaster bridge - why Elixir keeps the relevant machinery

Elixir is our language for expressing the program. It uses the Erlang runtime
and OTP facilities; changing the source language does not discard the process
and supervision model. [D1-D3, D9]

| Name              | Role in this remaster                                              |
| ----------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Elixir            | Modules, functions, pattern matching, and the syntax we write      |
| BEAM              | The runtime that executes processes and routes their messages      |
| Erlang/OTP        | Libraries and conventions for servers, supervision, and operations |
| Nx                | Tensor computations inside clearly defined numerical kernels       |
| Livebook and Kino | Executable explanations and inspection of results                  |

**Concurrency** means that multiple activities can make progress over overlapping
periods. **Parallelism** means that work actually executes simultaneously.
One BEAM process is not one operating-system process or one dedicated CPU core.
Creating more processes does not remove dependencies between computations.
[D1, D4]

Sher also invokes linguistic determinism to argue that a language affects the
ideas a programmer can comfortably express. We retain that as his motivation.
The supplied chapter does not present a comparative experiment establishing that
other languages prevent those ideas or that a process-per-neuron implementation
is always faster. [S5, pp. 143, 147]

## 5.3 The Conceptual Mapping of a NN to Erlang's Architecture

*Source: [S5], Section 5.3, pp. 147-149; Fig. 5.1 on p. 148.*

Sher's figure maps the **whole agent interface**, not only its internal neurons.
The sensor, neuron, and actuator nodes in the upper diagram become communicating
processes in the lower diagram; the connection pattern is preserved.

| Neural-system element in Fig. 5.1 | Process-oriented representation                     |
| --------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |
| Sensor                            | Process that obtains and encodes an observation     |
| Neuron                            | Process that holds parameters and transforms inputs |
| Connection                        | A destination identity and a message protocol       |
| Signal                            | A message containing the relevant numerical values  |
| Actuator                          | Process that interprets outputs as actions          |
| Network                           | A graph of these communicating components           |

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    subgraph Model[Conceptual neural system]
        S[Sensor] --> N1[Neuron 1]
        N1 --> N2[Neuron 2]
        N2 --> A[Actuator]
    end
    subgraph Program[Process-oriented implementation]
        PS[Sensor process] -->|input message| P1[Neuron process 1]
        P1 -->|signal message| P2[Neuron process 2]
        P2 -->|output message| PA[Actuator process]
    end
    S -. maps to .-> PS
    N1 -. maps to .-> P1
    N2 -. maps to .-> P2
    A -. maps to .-> PA
```

*Figure R5.1 - A smaller, newly drawn version of the architectural correspondence
in Sher's Fig. 5.1. The two-neuron chain is our illustration, not his topology.*

A logical connection does not require an operating-system pipe or a permanent
runtime link. In particular, a neural connection and an Erlang **process link**
serve different purposes: the former routes model signals; the latter relates
process lifetimes and exit signals. [D1, D4]

### Experiment 1 - A neuron with a mailbox

**Remaster implementation.** Start a process, send it a complete input vector,
and receive its output. Its parameters remain in the arguments of its receive
loop. A request reference identifies the reply; a monitor detects termination of
the expected output process. [D1, D4]

The computation is the familiar one:

$$
y=\tanh\left(\sum_i w_i x_i+b\right).
$$

The support module implements that calculation both with ordinary numbers and
with Nx. Nx is not needed to create, monitor, or supervise a process.

```elixir
defmodule Chapter05.Kernels do
  import Nx.Defn

  @spec valid_vector?(term()) :: boolean()
  def valid_vector?(values) do
    is_list(values) and values != [] and Enum.all?(values, &is_number/1)
  end

  @spec scalar([number()], [number()], number()) :: float()
  def scalar(inputs, weights, bias) do
    unless valid_vector?(inputs) and valid_vector?(weights) and
             length(inputs) == length(weights) and is_number(bias) do
      raise ArgumentError, "inputs and weights must be nonempty numeric lists of equal length"
    end

    z = Enum.zip(inputs, weights)
        |> Enum.reduce(bias, fn {x, w}, total -> total + x * w end)
    :math.tanh(z)
  end

  # x: {batch, inputs}; w1: {inputs}; b1, w2, b2: scalars.
  @spec pipeline(Nx.Tensor.t(), Nx.Tensor.t(), Nx.Tensor.t() | number(),
                 Nx.Tensor.t() | number(), Nx.Tensor.t() | number()) :: Nx.Tensor.t()
  defn pipeline(x, w1, b1, w2, b2) do
    hidden = Nx.tanh(Nx.dot(x, w1) + b1)
    Nx.tanh(hidden * w2 + b2)
  end
end

defmodule Chapter05.RawNeuron do
  @moduledoc false

  @spec start([number()], number(), :reply | pid()) :: pid()
  def start(weights, bias, output) do
    unless Chapter05.Kernels.valid_vector?(weights) and is_number(bias) and
             (output == :reply or is_pid(output)) do
      raise ArgumentError, "invalid neuron configuration"
    end

    owner = self()
    spawn(fn ->
      owner_monitor = Process.monitor(owner)
      loop(weights, bias, output, owner_monitor)
    end)
  end

  @spec predict(pid(), pid(), [number()]) :: {:ok, float()} | {:error, term()}
  def predict(entry, terminal, inputs) do
    request = make_ref()
    monitor = Process.monitor(terminal)
    send(entry, {:signal, self(), request, inputs})

    result =
      receive do
        {:output, ^request, ^terminal, value} -> {:ok, value}
        {:DOWN, ^monitor, :process, ^terminal, reason} -> {:error, {:down, reason}}
      after
        2_000 -> {:error, :timeout}
      end

    Process.demonitor(monitor, [:flush])
    result
  end

  @spec stop(pid()) :: :ok
  def stop(pid) do
    monitor = Process.monitor(pid)
    send(pid, :stop)

    receive do
      {:DOWN, ^monitor, :process, ^pid, _reason} -> :ok
    after
      1_000 ->
        Process.exit(pid, :kill)
        Chapter05.Check.down!(monitor, pid)
        :ok
    end
  end

  @spec loop([number()], number(), :reply | pid(), reference()) :: :ok
  defp loop(weights, bias, output, owner_monitor) do
    receive do
      {:signal, reply_to, request, inputs} ->
        value = Chapter05.Kernels.scalar(inputs, weights, bias)

        case output do
          :reply -> send(reply_to, {:output, request, self(), value})
          next_pid -> send(next_pid, {:signal, reply_to, request, [value]})
        end

        loop(weights, bias, output, owner_monitor)

      :stop ->
        :ok

      {:DOWN, ^owner_monitor, :process, _owner, _reason} ->
        :ok

      _other ->
        loop(weights, bias, output, owner_monitor)
    end
  end
end
```

```elixir
pid_single = Chapter05.RawNeuron.start([2.0, -0.5], -0.25, :reply)

{tbl_mailbox, checks_mailbox} =
  try do
    {:ok, first} = Chapter05.RawNeuron.predict(pid_single, pid_single, [0.5, -1.0])
    {:ok, second} = Chapter05.RawNeuron.predict(pid_single, pid_single, [0.0, 0.0])

    rows = [
      %{request: 1, process: inspect(pid_single), input: "[0.5, -1.0]", output: first},
      %{request: 2, process: inspect(pid_single), input: "[0.0, 0.0]", output: second}
    ]

    checks = [
      {abs(first - :math.tanh(1.25)) < 1.0e-12, "Mailbox neuron computes tanh(1.25)"},
      {abs(second - :math.tanh(-0.25)) < 1.0e-12, "Second request uses the same parameters"},
      {Process.alive?(pid_single), "Neuron remains alive between requests"}
    ]

    {rows, checks}
  after
    Chapter05.RawNeuron.stop(pid_single)
  end

checks_mailbox = checks_mailbox ++ [
  {not Process.alive?(pid_single), "Standalone mailbox neuron is cleaned up"}
]

Chapter05.Tables.new(tbl_mailbox, [:request, :process, :input, :output],
  "ch_05_tbl_01_mailbox.csv")
```

The first output should be about **0.848284**; the second about **-0.244919**.
Different inputs changed the output, not the weights.

The tail call to `loop/4` is the last action in each continuing branch: there is
no unfinished calculation waiting for the next iteration to return. The state is
passed explicitly. Tail recursion does not imply that an unconsumed mailbox is
bounded. [D1, D4]

**Protocol limits:** every message contains all inputs for one evaluation. We are
not yet collecting signals from multiple upstream neurons or defining recurrent
delays. A request timeout is not cancellation of a signal already sent. Reference
matching prevents a late reply being mistaken for a newer reply, but this small
raw protocol does not remove late replies automatically. The later examples use
OTP interfaces rather than treating this as a production RPC implementation.

### Experiment 2 - The same pipeline as processes and tensors

Connect a second neuron to the first. The first process now sends its result
**directly to the second process**. The notebook receives only the terminal reply.

$$
h=\tanh(2x_1-0.5x_2-0.25),\qquad
 y=\tanh(1.5h+0.1).
$$

The process graph and the batched tensor expression implement the same specified
computation. This is a numerical parity check, **not a speed benchmark**.

```elixir
inputs_pipeline = [[0.5, -1.0], [0.0, 0.0], [1.0, 1.0], [-1.0, 0.5]]
x_pipeline = Nx.tensor(inputs_pipeline, type: :f32)
w_pipeline = Nx.tensor([2.0, -0.5], type: :f32)

y_pipeline_nx =
  Chapter05.Kernels.pipeline(x_pipeline, w_pipeline, -0.25, 1.5, 0.1)
  |> Nx.to_flat_list()

pid_output = Chapter05.RawNeuron.start([1.5], 0.1, :reply)
pid_hidden = Chapter05.RawNeuron.start([2.0, -0.5], -0.25, pid_output)

{tbl_pipeline, checks_pipeline} =
  try do
    rows =
      Enum.zip(inputs_pipeline, y_pipeline_nx)
      |> Enum.map(fn {[x1, x2] = inputs, nx_output} ->
        {:ok, process_output} = Chapter05.RawNeuron.predict(pid_hidden, pid_output, inputs)
        scalar_hidden = Chapter05.Kernels.scalar(inputs, [2.0, -0.5], -0.25)
        scalar_output = Chapter05.Kernels.scalar([scalar_hidden], [1.5], 0.1)

        %{x1: x1, x2: x2, process_output: process_output,
          scalar_output: scalar_output, nx_output: nx_output,
          absolute_error: abs(process_output - nx_output)}
      end)

    checks = [
      {pid_hidden != pid_output, "Pipeline neurons are separate processes"},
      {Enum.all?(rows, &(abs(&1.process_output - &1.scalar_output) < 1.0e-12)),
       "Process pipeline agrees with scalar composition"},
      {Enum.all?(rows, &(&1.absolute_error < 1.0e-5)),
       "Process pipeline agrees with the batched Nx kernel"}
    ]
    {rows, checks}
  after
    Chapter05.RawNeuron.stop(pid_hidden)
    Chapter05.RawNeuron.stop(pid_output)
  end

checks_pipeline = checks_pipeline ++ [
  {not Process.alive?(pid_hidden) and not Process.alive?(pid_output),
   "Both pipeline processes are cleaned up"}
]

Chapter05.Tables.new(tbl_pipeline,
  [:x1, :x2, :process_output, :scalar_output, :nx_output, :absolute_error],
  "ch_05_tbl_02_pipeline_parity.csv")
```

### Remaster architecture - preserve the model, choose the granularity

Sher's process-per-neuron mapping is useful for understanding his design. Our
remaster also permits a process to own a **whole network, agent, or evaluation
job**, with Nx performing its internal numerical work. That is our implementation
choice, not a claim that Sher used Nx. [D1, D2, D10]

| Work                                              | Representation we can choose                        |
| ------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------- |
| Individual educational signal processors          | One process per neuron                              |
| Regular dense inference                           | A tensor kernel, optionally batched across examples |
| Independent candidate evaluations                 | Separate tasks or supervised workers                |
| Dynamic topology, lifecycle, experiment state     | Elixir data structures and explicit coordination    |
| Numerical mutation, distance measures, or ranking | Nx where shapes and semantics make it useful        |

**BEAM coordinates activities; Nx expresses numerical work.** This is not a rule
that generation must use BEAM and evaluation must use Nx. Nor is it a requirement
to create a process for every multiplication.

## 5.4 Robustness and Fault Tolerance in Computational Intelligence

*Source: [S5], Section 5.4, p. 149.*

Sher argues that a single bug should not bring down the entire computational
intelligence system. He proposes supervision trees and an overseeing component,
the **exoself**, that can restore failed elements. He also connects recovery to
the possibility of updating code during operation. [S5, p. 149]

### Remaster distinction - detection, restart, recovery, and repair

| Mechanism                            | What it does                                   | What it does not do automatically               |
| ------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |
| Monitor                              | Reports that a process terminated              | Restart it or recover its state                 |
| Supervisor                           | Restarts children according to policy          | Repair the bug that caused the crash            |
| Initialization or checkpoint loading | Reconstructs the chosen state                  | Restore information that was never retained     |
| Code replacement                     | Makes a new implementation available           | Prove correctness or migrate every state format |
| Retry policy                         | Decides whether work should be attempted again | Make external side effects exactly-once         |

These distinctions make Sher's architectural proposal executable. A supervisor
restarts a process from its configured start arguments; it does not copy the dead
process's last heap or mailbox. [D2-D4]

```mermaid
flowchart TB
    S[Supervisor: one_for_one] --> L[Neuron worker left]
    S --> R[Neuron worker right]
    O[Observer] -. monitor .-> L
    L -. termination notice .-> O
    S -->|restart same child specification| N[Replacement left worker]
```

*Figure R5.2 - Supervision and observation are separate relationships. The
replacement has a new PID. The right worker need not restart.*

### Experiment 3 - Restart is not memory restoration

**Remaster implementation.** `GenServer` supplies the request/reply loop. Our
callbacks define the neuron's calculation, runtime configuration, and state.
The supervisor controls its lifecycle. [D2, D3]

```elixir
defmodule Chapter05.Neuron do
  use GenServer

  @type state :: %{
    weights: [number()], bias: number(),
    calls: non_neg_integer(), revision: non_neg_integer()
  }

  @spec child_spec(map()) :: Supervisor.child_spec()
  def child_spec(config) do
    %{id: __MODULE__, start: {__MODULE__, :start_link, [config]},
      restart: :permanent, shutdown: 1_000, type: :worker}
  end

  @spec start_link(map()) :: GenServer.on_start()
  def start_link(config), do: GenServer.start_link(__MODULE__, config)

  @spec predict(pid(), [number()]) :: {:ok, float()} | {:error, :invalid_input}
  def predict(pid, inputs), do: GenServer.call(pid, {:predict, inputs}, 2_000)

  @spec snapshot(pid()) :: state()
  def snapshot(pid), do: GenServer.call(pid, :snapshot, 2_000)

  @spec configure(pid(), [number()], number()) :: :ok | {:error, :invalid_parameters}
  def configure(pid, weights, bias) do
    GenServer.call(pid, {:configure, weights, bias}, 2_000)
  end

  @impl true
  @spec init(map()) :: {:ok, state()} | {:stop, :invalid_parameters}
  def init(config) do
    weights = Map.fetch!(config, :weights)
    bias = Map.fetch!(config, :bias)

    if Chapter05.Kernels.valid_vector?(weights) and is_number(bias) do
      state = %{weights: weights, bias: bias, calls: 0, revision: 0}

      case config do
        %{observer: observer, tag: tag, id: id} ->
          send(observer, {:chapter05_started, tag, id, self()})
        _ -> :ok
      end

      {:ok, state}
    else
      {:stop, :invalid_parameters}
    end
  end

  @impl true
  @spec handle_call(term(), GenServer.from(), state()) :: {:reply, term(), state()}
  def handle_call({:predict, inputs}, _from, state) do
    if Chapter05.Kernels.valid_vector?(inputs) and length(inputs) == length(state.weights) do
      value = Chapter05.Kernels.scalar(inputs, state.weights, state.bias)
      {:reply, {:ok, value}, %{state | calls: state.calls + 1}}
    else
      {:reply, {:error, :invalid_input}, state}
    end
  end

  def handle_call(:snapshot, _from, state), do: {:reply, state, state}

  def handle_call({:configure, weights, bias}, _from, state) do
    if Chapter05.Kernels.valid_vector?(weights) and
         length(weights) == length(state.weights) and is_number(bias) do
      updated = %{state | weights: weights, bias: bias, revision: state.revision + 1}
      {:reply, :ok, updated}
    else
      {:reply, {:error, :invalid_parameters}, state}
    end
  end

  def handle_call(_request, _from, state), do: {:reply, {:error, :unknown_request}, state}
end
```

The next cell changes a live worker's weights, then deliberately terminates that
worker. Startup notifications provide synchronization; there is no assumption
that a fixed sleep is long enough for a restart.

```elixir
tag_recovery = make_ref()
config_left = %{weights: [1.0], bias: 0.0, observer: self(), tag: tag_recovery, id: :left}
config_right = %{weights: [-1.0], bias: 0.0, observer: self(), tag: tag_recovery, id: :right}

children = [
  Supervisor.child_spec({Chapter05.Neuron, config_left}, id: :left),
  Supervisor.child_spec({Chapter05.Neuron, config_right}, id: :right)
]

{:ok, pid_supervisor} = Supervisor.start_link(children,
  strategy: :one_for_one, max_restarts: 3, max_seconds: 5)

{tbl_recovery, checks_recovery} =
  try do
    pid_left = Chapter05.Check.started!(tag_recovery, :left)
    pid_right = Chapter05.Check.started!(tag_recovery, :right)
    initial = Chapter05.Neuron.snapshot(pid_left)

    {:ok, _value} = Chapter05.Neuron.predict(pid_left, [0.5])
    :ok = Chapter05.Neuron.configure(pid_left, [2.0], 0.1)
    configured = Chapter05.Neuron.snapshot(pid_left)

    {:error, :invalid_input} = Chapter05.Neuron.predict(pid_left, [0.0, 1.0])
    after_invalid = Chapter05.Neuron.snapshot(pid_left)

    monitor_left = Process.monitor(pid_left)
    Process.exit(pid_left, :kill)
    reason = Chapter05.Check.down!(monitor_left, pid_left)
    pid_replacement = Chapter05.Check.started!(tag_recovery, :left)
    restarted = Chapter05.Neuron.snapshot(pid_replacement)

    current_right =
      Supervisor.which_children(pid_supervisor)
      |> Enum.find_value(fn
        {:right, pid, :worker, _modules} -> pid
        _ -> nil
      end)

    rows = [
      %{stage: "initial", pid: inspect(pid_left), weights: inspect(initial.weights),
        bias: initial.bias, calls: initial.calls, revision: initial.revision},
      %{stage: "configured in place", pid: inspect(pid_left), weights: inspect(configured.weights),
        bias: configured.bias, calls: configured.calls, revision: configured.revision},
      %{stage: "after restart", pid: inspect(pid_replacement), weights: inspect(restarted.weights),
        bias: restarted.bias, calls: restarted.calls, revision: restarted.revision}
    ]

    checks = [
      {configured.weights == [2.0] and configured.revision == 1,
       "Live configuration updates parameters"},
      {configured.calls == 1, "Worker records one successful request"},
      {after_invalid == configured, "Invalid input returns an error without changing state"},
      {reason == :killed, "Monitor observes the intentional worker termination"},
      {pid_replacement != pid_left, "Supervisor starts a replacement with a new PID"},
      {restarted == initial, "Restart restores initialization, not the latest runtime state"},
      {current_right == pid_right and Process.alive?(pid_right),
       "one_for_one leaves the sibling worker running"},
      {Process.alive?(pid_supervisor), "Supervisor survives the worker failure"}
    ]
    {rows, checks}
  after
    Supervisor.stop(pid_supervisor, :normal, 2_000)
  end

checks_recovery = checks_recovery ++ [
  {not Process.alive?(pid_supervisor), "Recovery demonstration supervisor is cleaned up"}
]

Chapter05.Tables.new(tbl_recovery, [:stage, :pid, :weights, :bias, :calls, :revision],
  "ch_05_tbl_03_recovery.csv")
```

The restarted worker should have weight **1.0**, bias **0.0**, **0** calls, and
revision **0**. Its runtime weight **2.0** was not part of the child start
specification and was not checkpointed. This is the intended lesson, not a failed
recovery test.

**For our neuroevolution platform:** preserve the genotype and evaluation policy
outside an expendable phenotype. Decide separately whether to reconstruct the
phenotype, resume a checkpoint, or restart the entire episode. After a restart,
connections holding an old PID need a routing or reconstruction policy. Supervisor
child IDs identify specifications; they do not automatically repair every PID
stored elsewhere. [D3, D4]

### Experiment 4 - Replace code without restarting the worker

Updating weights with `configure/3` changed **data**. It did not replace code.
Here we replace the implementation of a dedicated activation module while a
worker stays alive. Its calls use `apply(module, function, arguments)`, a remote
module call that resolves the current implementation. [D5, D11]

This is a deliberately small demonstration: the worker state format does not
change, requests are completed before replacement, and there is only one host.
It is not a complete release-upgrade procedure or an autonomous self-repair system.

```elixir
defmodule Chapter05.LiveHost do
  use GenServer
  @type state :: %{kernel: module(), calls: non_neg_integer()}

  @spec child_spec(module()) :: Supervisor.child_spec()
  def child_spec(kernel) do
    %{id: __MODULE__, start: {__MODULE__, :start_link, [kernel]},
      restart: :permanent, shutdown: 1_000, type: :worker}
  end

  @spec start_link(module()) :: GenServer.on_start()
  def start_link(kernel), do: GenServer.start_link(__MODULE__, kernel)

  @spec run(pid(), number()) :: map()
  def run(pid, x), do: GenServer.call(pid, {:run, x}, 2_000)

  @impl true
  @spec init(module()) :: {:ok, state()}
  def init(kernel), do: {:ok, %{kernel: kernel, calls: 0}}

  @impl true
  @spec handle_call({:run, number()}, GenServer.from(), state()) :: {:reply, map(), state()}
  def handle_call({:run, x}, _from, state) do
    result = %{pid: self(), calls: state.calls + 1,
      version: apply(state.kernel, :version, []),
      output: apply(state.kernel, :activate, [x])}
    {:reply, result, %{state | calls: state.calls + 1}}
  end
end
```

```elixir
# These are fixed notebook sources. Never compile untrusted input in this way.
source_linear = ~S"""
defmodule Chapter05.HotActivation do
  @spec version() :: :linear
  def version(), do: :linear

  @spec activate(number()) :: number()
  def activate(x), do: x
end
"""

source_tanh = ~S"""
defmodule Chapter05.HotActivation do
  @spec version() :: :tanh
  def version(), do: :tanh

  @spec activate(number()) :: float()
  def activate(x), do: :math.tanh(x)
end
"""

Code.compile_string(source_linear)
{:ok, pid_live_host} = Chapter05.LiveHost.start_link(Chapter05.HotActivation)

{tbl_upgrade, checks_upgrade} =
  try do
    before_upgrade = Chapter05.LiveHost.run(pid_live_host, 1.0)
    Code.compile_string(source_tanh)
    after_upgrade = Chapter05.LiveHost.run(pid_live_host, 1.0)

    rows =
      Enum.map([before_upgrade, after_upgrade], fn result ->
        %{version: result.version, process: inspect(result.pid),
          calls: result.calls, input: 1.0, output: result.output}
      end)

    checks = [
      {before_upgrade.pid == after_upgrade.pid, "Code replacement retains the worker PID"},
      {before_upgrade.calls == 1 and after_upgrade.calls == 2,
       "Worker state survives the activation-code replacement"},
      {before_upgrade.version == :linear and before_upgrade.output == 1.0,
       "First call uses the original implementation"},
      {after_upgrade.version == :tanh and abs(after_upgrade.output - :math.tanh(1.0)) < 1.0e-12,
       "Next remote call uses the replacement implementation"}
    ]
    {rows, checks}
  after
    GenServer.stop(pid_live_host, :normal, 2_000)
  end

checks_upgrade = checks_upgrade ++ [
  {not Process.alive?(pid_live_host), "Code-replacement host is cleaned up"}
]

Chapter05.Tables.new(tbl_upgrade, [:version, :process, :calls, :input, :output],
  "ch_05_tbl_04_code_replacement.csv")
```

The output changes from **1.0** to about **0.761594**, while the process identity
stays the same and the call count increases.

BEAM supports current and old versions of a module. Code replacement therefore
requires care about processes still executing old code, especially before further
replacements. Our activation calls have returned before each replacement; the
example is not a general guarantee that repeatedly reloading arbitrary modules
is safe. [D5]

When a GenServer's **state representation** also changes, an upgrade plan may
need `code_change/3` and coordinated OTP upgrade handling. Simply calling
`Code.compile_string/1` does not automatically migrate all running server states.
Our example avoids that issue by keeping the host state unchanged. [D2, D5, D11]

## 5.5 I've Done This Once Before; And We Are On Our Way

*Source: [S5], Section 5.5, pp. 149-150.*

Sher closes the foundations by promising a step-by-step neuroevolutionary
platform. He points forward to artificial-life agents, recognition of patterns in
financial charts, and flexible learning networks applicable to circuits and other
problems. These are the book's announced destinations; this chapter does not
supply experimental results for them. [S5, pp. 149-150]

In our remaster, the next step is a concrete feedforward network. Before building
it, connect this chapter's architecture to the GES framework we established.

| GES operation | Information it owns                                | Possible execution unit                                      |
| ------------- | -------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Generation    | Parent designs, mutation settings, random state    | Coordinator plus numerical or structural operators           |
| Evaluation    | Episode state, observations, outputs, measurements | Isolated job, supervised agent, or numerical batch           |
| Selection     | Measured candidates and an explicit policy         | Coordinator choosing parents, survivors, and archive entries |

A failed evaluation is an **operational outcome**, not automatically a low fitness
score. Specify whether to retry, reject an invalid candidate, or mark the run as
incomplete. Otherwise selection can become biased by infrastructure behavior.
This is a remaster design rule, not a rule specified in Sher's Chapter 5.

### Experiment 5 - A failed evaluation does not erase the other results

Evaluate four hand-designed one-neuron candidates against the target
$y=\tanh(x)$. A fifth job deliberately exits. Successful jobs report negative
mean squared error, so higher fitness is better:

$$
F(w,b)=-\frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^{N}
\left[\tanh(wx_i+b)-\tanh(x_i)\right]^2.
$$

The target formula is known because this is a controlled plumbing test, not an
unknown-function discovery experiment. There is no mutation or evolutionary
search here. Selection simply chooses the highest-scoring successful candidate.

`Task.Supervisor.async_stream_nolink/4` separates task failures from the caller.
`ordered: true` lets us pair each result with its candidate in the original order;
it does **not** require tasks to finish in that order. Concurrency is bounded at
two jobs. [D12]

```elixir
defmodule Chapter05.Evaluation do
  @moduledoc false
  @type candidate :: %{id: String.t(), weight: number(), bias: number(), fail?: boolean()}
  @type sample :: {number(), number()}

  @spec score(candidate(), [sample()]) :: float()
  def score(%{fail?: true}, _samples), do: exit(:intentional_evaluation_failure)

  def score(candidate, samples) do
    if samples == [], do: raise(ArgumentError, "evaluation needs at least one sample")

    squared_error =
      Enum.reduce(samples, 0.0, fn {x, target}, total ->
        prediction = Chapter05.Kernels.scalar([x], [candidate.weight], candidate.bias)
        total + (prediction - target) * (prediction - target)
      end)

    -squared_error / length(samples)
  end

  @spec run([candidate()], [sample()]) :: [map()]
  def run(candidates, samples) do
    {:ok, supervisor} = Task.Supervisor.start_link()

    try do
      results =
        Task.Supervisor.async_stream_nolink(supervisor, candidates,
          fn candidate -> score(candidate, samples) end,
          max_concurrency: 2, ordered: true, timeout: 5_000, on_timeout: :kill_task)
        |> Enum.to_list()

      Enum.zip(candidates, results)
      |> Enum.map(fn
        {candidate, {:ok, fitness}} ->
          %{id: candidate.id, weight: candidate.weight, bias: candidate.bias,
            status: "ok", fitness: fitness, reason: ""}

        {candidate, {:exit, reason}} ->
          %{id: candidate.id, weight: candidate.weight, bias: candidate.bias,
            status: "failed", fitness: nil, reason: inspect(reason)}
      end)
    after
      Supervisor.stop(supervisor, :normal, 2_000)
    end
  end
end

candidates = [
  %{id: "flat", weight: 0.0, bias: 0.0, fail?: false},
  %{id: "shallow", weight: 0.5, bias: 0.0, fail?: false},
  %{id: "matching", weight: 1.0, bias: 0.0, fail?: false},
  %{id: "steep", weight: 2.0, bias: 0.0, fail?: false},
  %{id: "fault", weight: 1.0, bias: 0.0, fail?: true}
]

samples = Enum.map([-1.0, -0.5, 0.0, 0.5, 1.0], fn x -> {x, :math.tanh(x)} end)
tbl_evaluation = Chapter05.Evaluation.run(candidates, samples)

successful = Enum.filter(tbl_evaluation, &(&1.status == "ok"))
failed = Enum.filter(tbl_evaluation, &(&1.status == "failed"))
selected = successful |> Enum.sort_by(fn row -> {-row.fitness, row.id} end) |> hd()

checks_evaluation = [
  {length(tbl_evaluation) == 5, "Every candidate retains an identifiable result"},
  {length(successful) == 4 and length(failed) == 1, "One failing job does not erase four successes"},
  {hd(failed).id == "fault" and is_nil(hd(failed).fitness), "Failure is recorded separately from fitness"},
  {Enum.all?(successful, &(&1.fitness <= 0.0)), "Fitness uses negative mean squared error"},
  {selected.id == "matching" and abs(selected.fitness) < 1.0e-12,
   "Selection chooses the matching successful candidate"}
]

Chapter05.Tables.new(tbl_evaluation, [:id, :weight, :bias, :status, :fitness, :reason],
  "ch_05_tbl_05_evaluation_jobs.csv")
```

The failed job is recorded, not silently assigned a perfect score of zero. This
example's explicit policy excludes failed jobs from selection; it does not retry
them. A production experiment needs its own documented failure policy.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    G[Generation supplies candidate designs] --> E[Bounded evaluation jobs]
    E --> R[Results retain candidate IDs]
    R --> S[Selection policy]
    R --> F[Failure records]
    S --> P[Parents and survivors]
    S --> A[Archive policy]
    P --> G
```

*Figure R5.3 - Our GES coordination sketch. The experiment implements evaluation
and a simple selection decision, not the generation loop or an archive.*

### What remains to be built

| This chapter demonstrates        | Later work must make explicit                              |
| -------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- |
| A small process graph            | Genotype-driven construction and wiring of larger networks |
| Complete-vector messages         | Fan-in synchronization, time steps, and recurrent delays   |
| A supervised restart             | Episode recovery and connection reconstruction             |
| A stateless function replacement | Coordinated application upgrades and state migrations      |
| Local concurrent jobs            | Distribution, reproducibility, budgets, and persistence    |

### Executable checks

Run after the five experiments. Numerical agreement, lifecycle events, and
policy decisions are checked separately. This is a teaching test suite, not a
stress test or a proof of general fault tolerance.

```elixir
csv_probe = Chapter05.Tables.csv([%{label: "a,\"b\"", value: nil}], [:label, :value])
markdown_probe = Chapter05.Tables.markdown([%{value: "left|right"}], [:value])

checks_format = [
  {is_binary(csv_probe), "CSV export returns a binary"},
  {String.contains?(csv_probe, "\"a,\"\"b\"\"\""), "CSV quotes commas and embedded quotes"},
  {String.ends_with?(csv_probe, "\r\n"), "CSV has a terminating record separator"},
  {String.contains?(markdown_probe, "left\\|right"), "Computed Markdown tables escape pipes"}
]

tbl_checks =
  (checks_mailbox ++ checks_pipeline ++ checks_recovery ++ checks_upgrade ++
     checks_evaluation ++ checks_format)
  |> Enum.map(&Chapter05.Check.row!/1)

Chapter05.Tables.new(tbl_checks, [:check, :result], "ch_05_tbl_06_checks.csv")
```

### Exercises

| Change                                                | Inspect                                                            |
| ----------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Change a mailbox neuron's bias                        | Does the message protocol change, or only the numerical response?  |
| Add an extra input to `Neuron.predict/2`              | Is the failure returned as data? Does the worker remain available? |
| Remove the runtime `configure/3` call                 | Which differences between initial and restarted state disappear?   |
| Replace `:one_for_one` with `:one_for_all`            | Why does the sibling-PID assertion now fail?                       |
| Use the same activation formula in both code versions | Does the output still provide evidence that code changed?          |
| Change the task-selection failure policy              | Which candidates are eligible, and what does a missing score mean? |

For the supervision-strategy exercise, changed outcomes are expected: it is not
an instruction to weaken the original assertions until any behavior passes.

### Takeaway

**A neural design, a process graph, and a tensor program are related
representations, not interchangeable guarantees.** Use processes to express
independent activities and recovery boundaries. Use numerical kernels to express
regular arithmetic. Keep the semantics of the neural model and the evaluation
protocol explicit across both.

**Next: Chapter 6 - Developing a Feedforward Neural Network.** We will turn
these architectural choices into a network construction and execution model.

